Rollout patch 0007: opt-in WMMA QSA scorer for gfx1100 (rocWMMA rocm-7.1.1).

The portable scorer (patch 0001) is one sequential F32 dot per (row, head) -
correct by construction, slow by design. This patch adds a second, OPT-IN arm:
score_wmma_kernel, one wave32 per 16 pooled rows, computing the 128-deep dots
with rocWMMA 16x16x16 fp16 fragments and f32 accumulation (gfx1100 native WMMA;
vendored rocm-7.1.1 headers - v0.8 cannot compile mma_sync for gfx1100, measured,
PORTING.md 12g). Inputs convert to fp16 round-to-nearest: the same 10-mantissa-bit
loss the tf32 path simulates, with pooled/query magnitudes O(1) by construction
(RMS-normalized rows times w_k_norm), far from fp16 range/subnormal edges. Rows
past `full` are zero-filled before the fragment loads; the query heads are padded
4 -> 16 in shared memory so the col_major fragment stays in bounds; the epilogue
is the PORTABLE epilogue verbatim (per-head ReLU, ordered head sum, bias, 1e9
tail, writes in [0,n)). Dispatch is a separate atomic (set_wmma), default OFF -
the portable path remains the default and the reference. The error bound this arm
must meet (fp16 conversion + order-agnostic f32 accumulation + shared epilogue)
is derived BEFORE this kernel in PORTING.md 12g and asserted by
tests/rocm/qsa_score_wmma_parity.cpp against a float64 oracle and a cross-check
against the portable arm. Engine wiring is deliberately NOT part of this patch:
native_qsa_score is test-only upstream, and the engine's fast-select path uses a
different capacity (max_blocks = max_cells/4 + 2) and a separate spare-key buffer
- wiring is a follow-up gated on the bench numbers.

